			<div class="content" id="post-content-2679617">
									

<p>Couple areas that could be touched up a bit:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>In the event that security around SCP-2973 is compromised, on-site personnel are authorized to destroy SCP-2973-1 either by destroying the device it is contained in or by powering down the device. It is necessary to destroy the device in the case that SCP-2973 has rendered this impossible to do digitally.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Try:</p>
<table class="wiki-content-table">
<tr>
<td>In the event that security around SCP-2973 is compromised, on-site personnel must power down the affected devices. In the event that SCP-2973 has rendered this impossible, physical destruction is permitted.</td>
</tr>
</table>
<blockquote>
<p>require the approval</p>
</blockquote>
<p>requires</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The toy itself displays no anomalous properties at the present although it may be capable of producing unique instances of SCP-2973-1.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't understand the purpose of this line. Is there only one -1 (excluding the test)? We don't really see 'unique instances' appearing outside the original. You never mention what happens if -1 is outside the 2973 screen and the device is power-cycled. How many can exist at once (if any)?</p>
<p>A better way to do this would be to simply say: 'The toy itself displays no anomalous properties aside from the generation of SCP-2973-1 instances.' Instead of implying multiple unique instances that you never mention again. Also, you never say what happens to the devices that are powered down. Do they stop smelling? Is -1 eradicated from the device?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2973-1 can exit the boundaries of SCP-2973's screen to appear on other electronic display.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I know you say 10m in the containment procedures, but you never actually state how far away a screen needs to be for this to activate.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>While SCP-2973-1 is inside of a display screen outside of SCP-2973</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think 'While SCP-2973-1 is inside device aside from SCP-2973' would flow better.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>While inside of a display screen SCP-2973-1 is capable of interacting with file thumbnails and utilizing them in the typical manner that their thumbnails would in a physical reality</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is clunky as hell. 'interacting with file thumbnails as if they were physical objects' shouold get the idea across.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-2973-1 can then be picked up and manipulated in this manner and can be flung by the user into another display screen using this momentum.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I kinda don't get the point of this line, either.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
I'm nthing the opinion that the final test log is ridiculously incompetent. I could definitely see something with a D-class, but even then I think the death would be cheap. What you could try <em>instead</em> is to have the feed show the mauling/murder&#8230;.and the dude is totally fine IRL.
<p>Taking that even further, you'd get bonus points with me if they let the video run for a few more minutes, and the victim reanimates (displaying characteristics similar to subjects infected with SCP-008) and wanders out of the room. Never to be seen IRL or any display.</p>
<p>Triple bonus points if this is titled 'Zombigotchi'.</p>
<p>I'm currently undecided&#8230;leaning towards a downvote. Depending on how you fix this up, I could see myself swinging towards a +1.</p>

							</div>

								
					
