			<div class="content" id="post-content-2680248">
									

<p>Since we are on the subject of pennies and chemistry, I have to tell this story. A few years after the introduction of zinc pennies, one of my chemistry professors came to class early and filled a 2L beaker with water. He started throwing pennies into the water, one after another, and each one would naturally sink to the bottom. Once a crowd of curious students had gathered to see what was going on, he threw a penny that <em>floated</em>, and everybody freaked out.</p>
<p>He had earlier taken a zinc penny, scratched off a tiny bit of the copper coating, and used hydrochloric acid to dissolve away the zinc. This left a paper thin shell of copper than looked exactly like a normal penny. It's a super cool party trick if you ever find yourself with a surplus of hydrochloric acid.</p>

