<p>Ooh, actually copper doesn't have a smell, as such. What happens when you rub coins on your skin is that the copper or iron in the coins catalyzes chemical reactions that change the oils on your hands into ketones and/or aldehydes. So you're not smelling the coins, you're smelling altered body odor.<br />
Source: uh&#8230; I've misplaced it. Will update w/source later.</p>
<p>Edit: This <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oct-1-en-3-one">Wikipedia</a> page describes one of the more common &quot;metal smell&quot; compounds. Notice from the chemical formula that it does not contain Cu.</p>

