														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2680088">
									

<p>Oh, I'm not against the phrase &quot;smell of copper&quot;, I'm just pointing out that the smell is only produced by skin contact with the metal. If you found a chunk of copper out in the desert, it would have no smell until you picked it up.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Chemistry&nbsp;inside</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>(lipid peroxide from skin) + (free H+) + (metal ion) &#8212;&gt; (smellable ketone) + water + (metal ion with one fewer electron)<br />
CH<sub>3</sub>CH=CHC(=<strong>OOH</strong>)(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>n</sub>CH<sub>3</sub> + <strong>H<sup>+</sup></strong> + <strong>Fe<sup>2+</sup></strong> &#8212;&gt; CH<sub>2</sub>=CHC(=<strong>O</strong>)(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>n</sub>CH<sub>3</sub> + <strong>H<sub>2</sub>O</strong> + <strong>Fe<sup>3+</sup></strong></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>&#8230;but since we don't really know what the Tamagotchi is doing to the robot, there's any number of ways that a similar reaction could occur, so I'm fine with it, especially since Faminepulse has explained the artistic why.</p>

