														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2680129">
									

<p>Its basically <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cracking_(chemistry)#Catalytic_methods">catalytic cracking</a> via <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Redox">redox</a> reactions. That article actually describes it pretty well: The &quot;sharp&quot; part are the peroxide reactions. (Those are why you taste metal when you get badly shocked- does the same thing via electrolysis.)</p>
<p>The component from rust or patina (my salvage yard smell) is basically the smell of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Organometallic_chemistry">organmetallic chemistry</a>. Mix metal, carbon compounds, and pretty much anything from sunlight to acid salts and you get all sorts of cool cyclic reactions and metallic ion-bearing carbon compounds like hemoglobin, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metallocene">metallocenes</a>, and quasi-stable Cu-R ligands. And apparently organophosphines. (Hooray for favorite smells classified as chemical weapons! X3)</p>

							</div>

								
